1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:08,000
America is on the move in the most epic way.

2
00:00:08,001 --> 00:00:13,840
The United States has invested as much as $1.6 trillion a year

3
00:00:13,841 --> 00:00:18,800
in logistics, which is kind of a fancy way of saying transport.

4
00:00:18,801 --> 00:00:23,200
That's trillion with a capital T. But that's not the only number

5
00:00:23,201 --> 00:00:26,800
related to America in Motion that breaks my brain.

6
00:00:26,801 --> 00:00:31,180
Do you realize that 430 million pieces of mail

7
00:00:31,181 --> 00:00:33,380
are delivered every single day?

8
00:00:33,381 --> 00:00:38,880
Public transport makes 10 billion trips every year.

9
00:00:38,881 --> 00:00:42,120
There are freight trains that are three miles long.

10
00:00:42,121 --> 00:00:45,320
There are cargo ships that are 1,300 feet long.

11
00:00:45,321 --> 00:00:48,520
That's like a floating Empire State Building.

12
00:00:48,521 --> 00:00:52,260
But these modern, massive marvels that breathe life

13
00:00:52,261 --> 00:00:55,760
and give oxygen to our economy, they're not just brute beasts.

14
00:00:55,761 --> 00:00:58,700
Every year, they continue to advance, to innovate,

15
00:00:58,701 --> 00:01:01,640
becoming more aerodynamic, more intelligent,

16
00:01:01,641 --> 00:01:05,180
and they're always full of surprises.

17
00:01:05,181 --> 00:01:08,040
Today, I'm at the Daimler Truck Manufacturing Center

18
00:01:08,041 --> 00:01:10,280
in Cleveland, North Carolina.

19
00:01:10,281 --> 00:01:14,720
It's spread over 1.2 million square feet.

20
00:01:14,721 --> 00:01:19,400
We'll get an inside look at how 1,700 workers build

21
00:01:19,401 --> 00:01:23,600
their Freightliner trucks that are essential to our economy

22
00:01:23,601 --> 00:01:27,500
and ship nearly everything across the country.

23
00:01:27,501 --> 00:01:32,080
Oh, and did I mention this is the first time in history

24
00:01:32,081 --> 00:01:37,320
that Daimler has ever let anyone inside their factory to film?

25
00:01:37,321 --> 00:01:41,320
Then we'll see how one company is making a flying machine

26
00:01:41,321 --> 00:01:45,680
that costs the same as a high-end pickup truck.

27
00:01:45,681 --> 00:01:49,020
Plus, we'll get a glimpse of what transport was like

28
00:01:49,021 --> 00:01:53,200
in the past and what it could be in the future.

29
00:01:53,600 --> 00:01:58,600
How will the latest technology allow us to go further faster

30
00:01:58,601 --> 00:02:00,600
than ever before?

31
00:02:00,601 --> 00:02:01,600
I'm going to find out.

32
00:02:01,601 --> 00:02:04,600
So grab your seat, and let's get the truck out of here.

33
00:02:04,601 --> 00:02:07,600
This is Modern Marvel's Moving America.

34
00:02:07,601 --> 00:02:08,600
America.

35
00:02:22,540 --> 00:02:25,260
Now, if you've ever been driving down the freeway

36
00:02:25,261 --> 00:02:28,520
and seen a massive truck towering over you,

37
00:02:28,521 --> 00:02:32,060
there is an awfully good chance it was made by Daimler.

38
00:02:32,061 --> 00:02:37,600
In fact, Daimler trucks alone deliver nearly 3 billion tons

39
00:02:37,601 --> 00:02:40,600
every single year.

40
00:02:40,601 --> 00:02:42,600
Daimler's footprint in North America

41
00:02:42,601 --> 00:02:45,100
includes a number of iconic brands,

42
00:02:45,101 --> 00:02:48,520
but the Freightliner is a creme de la creme,

43
00:02:48,521 --> 00:02:52,940
accounting for one out of every three truck sales in America.

44
00:02:52,941 --> 00:02:56,580
Now, it all started in the 1930s when Leland James,

45
00:02:56,581 --> 00:02:59,460
president of Consolidated Freightways,

46
00:02:59,461 --> 00:03:03,460
approached manufacturers about building truck parts

47
00:03:03,461 --> 00:03:06,960
with lightweight aluminum instead of the traditional steel.

48
00:03:06,961 --> 00:03:10,300
When his idea was met with skepticism and resistance,

49
00:03:10,400 --> 00:03:12,400
he hired his own team of engineers,

50
00:03:12,500 --> 00:03:15,200
and he built the truck his way.

51
00:03:15,300 --> 00:03:16,700
The result?

52
00:03:16,800 --> 00:03:19,040
Lighter weight meant they could haul more freight

53
00:03:19,140 --> 00:03:22,740
with every load, and it was an instant success.

54
00:03:22,840 --> 00:03:26,420
Daimler Trucks acquired the company in 1981

55
00:03:26,520 --> 00:03:30,620
and has since built the brand to the top choice for trucking.

56
00:03:33,320 --> 00:03:36,160
Today, the Cleveland, North Carolina factory

57
00:03:36,161 --> 00:03:40,500
is Daimler Trucks' largest truck manufacturing facility

58
00:03:40,501 --> 00:03:41,800
in America.

59
00:03:41,801 --> 00:03:45,800
And inside, 1,700 workers build trucks.

60
00:03:45,801 --> 00:03:50,800
And these long haulers are loaded with advanced technology

61
00:03:50,801 --> 00:03:55,720
and are built to last for a million miles or more.

62
00:03:55,721 --> 00:04:00,180
That's enough to drive around planet Earth 40 times.

63
00:04:00,181 --> 00:04:02,180
Adam, how you doing, boy?

64
00:04:02,181 --> 00:04:03,180
Good to see you.

65
00:04:03,181 --> 00:04:04,180
Thank you for having me.

66
00:04:04,181 --> 00:04:05,560
This place is amazing.

67
00:04:05,561 --> 00:04:08,800
and massive, and I want to know everything.

68
00:04:08,900 --> 00:04:09,800
You got it.

69
00:04:09,900 --> 00:04:13,760
Each truck Daimler produces is a custom order,

70
00:04:13,860 --> 00:04:17,540
meaning they come in different shapes, sizes,

71
00:04:17,640 --> 00:04:20,980
and configurations chosen by the customer.

72
00:04:21,080 --> 00:04:23,420
They can be built to handle different jobs

73
00:04:23,520 --> 00:04:27,520
and be outfitted with different options inside,

74
00:04:27,620 --> 00:04:29,680
like places to sleep and eat.

75
00:04:29,780 --> 00:04:34,820
Some are even better equipped in a New York City apartment.

76
00:04:34,821 --> 00:04:38,820
So every seven minutes, a truck rolls off the line.

77
00:04:38,821 --> 00:04:40,620
Sorry, sorry.

78
00:04:40,621 --> 00:04:41,820
Every seven minutes?

79
00:04:41,821 --> 00:04:43,820
Every seven minutes, a truck rolls off the line.

80
00:04:43,821 --> 00:04:47,240
You're producing roughly eight trucks an hour.

81
00:04:47,241 --> 00:04:48,240
You got it.

82
00:04:48,241 --> 00:04:49,800
So will you take me through the making

83
00:04:49,801 --> 00:04:51,240
of the iconic Freightliner?

84
00:04:51,241 --> 00:04:52,240
Absolutely. Let's do it.

85
00:04:52,241 --> 00:04:54,240
Let's do it.

86
00:04:54,241 --> 00:04:59,020
Each of Daimler's trucks take about 3,000 parts to build.

87
00:04:59,021 --> 00:05:01,940
But because each truck is custom,

88
00:05:01,941 --> 00:05:06,580
There's about 44,000 different possible parts that could be

89
00:05:06,581 --> 00:05:07,580
used.

90
00:05:07,581 --> 00:05:13,180
And this is the station where the high volume parts are stored.

91
00:05:13,181 --> 00:05:20,460
It's 26 feet high and 270 feet in length, longer than a Manhattan

92
00:05:20,461 --> 00:05:23,060
city block running north to south.

93
00:05:23,061 --> 00:05:25,860
Is there an automated system that selects it and brings it

94
00:05:25,861 --> 00:05:26,860
back?

95
00:05:26,861 --> 00:05:28,140
Yes.

96
00:05:28,141 --> 00:05:30,740
So this is like an automated truck building vending machine?

97
00:05:30,741 --> 00:05:31,940
Exactly.

98
00:05:31,941 --> 00:05:36,640
A high-volume part will be needed on nearly every truck Daimler builds,

99
00:05:36,980 --> 00:05:39,180
so of course they buy them in bulk.

100
00:05:39,520 --> 00:05:43,620
But in order to avoid the bulk parts clogging up the production line,

101
00:05:43,621 --> 00:05:48,020
they break them down into smaller boxes they call totes.

102
00:05:48,360 --> 00:05:52,100
The totes are then fed into the automated storage system

103
00:05:52,101 --> 00:05:54,560
where they'll stay until needed,

104
00:05:54,920 --> 00:05:58,240
at which point the system accesses the tote

105
00:05:58,241 --> 00:06:00,580
and spits it out to be delivered to the line

106
00:06:00,581 --> 00:06:03,540
where the parts are needed, when they're needed.

107
00:06:03,541 --> 00:06:05,240
It's starting to make sense to me

108
00:06:05,241 --> 00:06:08,580
how you guys are able to have a new custom truck

109
00:06:08,581 --> 00:06:10,580
roll off the line every seven minutes,

110
00:06:10,581 --> 00:06:12,920
because now you know where the things are.

111
00:06:12,921 --> 00:06:14,920
Now, I know you've got a lot of trucks to build

112
00:06:14,921 --> 00:06:16,960
and a lot of materials to get back to,

113
00:06:16,961 --> 00:06:18,460
so I'm gonna let you get back to it.

114
00:06:18,461 --> 00:06:19,920
Will you take me on to the next step?

115
00:06:19,921 --> 00:06:20,920
Absolutely.

116
00:06:20,921 --> 00:06:22,060
Yes, sir.

117
00:06:22,061 --> 00:06:23,260
Thank you.

118
00:06:23,261 --> 00:06:26,140
There's really no better place to learn about trucks

119
00:06:26,141 --> 00:06:31,140
than here at Daimler, because over 120 years ago,

120
00:06:31,141 --> 00:06:34,140
their namesake, Gottlieb Daimler,

121
00:06:34,141 --> 00:06:37,560
invented the very first motorized truck

122
00:06:37,561 --> 00:06:39,560
by taking a horse-drawn cart

123
00:06:39,561 --> 00:06:43,560
and giving it a four-horsepower engine.

124
00:06:43,561 --> 00:06:45,560
Today, the freight trucking market

125
00:06:45,561 --> 00:06:49,400
is worth over $700 billion.

126
00:06:49,401 --> 00:06:52,840
You have to think about railroad tracks and roads

127
00:06:52,841 --> 00:06:55,840
as a circulatory system of the United States.

128
00:06:55,841 --> 00:06:59,340
And what trucks do is they've enabled the last mile,

129
00:06:59,341 --> 00:07:03,780
where you can move anything right to a specific point

130
00:07:03,781 --> 00:07:05,080
on the planet.

131
00:07:05,081 --> 00:07:09,780
However, in the US, it wasn't until after World War I

132
00:07:09,781 --> 00:07:14,160
that trucks grew in demand, after extensive use

133
00:07:14,161 --> 00:07:15,500
by the military.

134
00:07:15,501 --> 00:07:17,300
Once the interstate system expanded

135
00:07:17,301 --> 00:07:21,160
across the United States, trucks became the preferred mechanism

136
00:07:21,161 --> 00:07:23,260
for moving a lot of cargo.

137
00:07:23,261 --> 00:07:26,600
And certain movies and songs came along

138
00:07:26,601 --> 00:07:29,300
that popularized the idea of a road cowboy,

139
00:07:29,301 --> 00:07:32,000
a person who could get in the cab of a truck

140
00:07:32,001 --> 00:07:33,500
and travel great distances.

141
00:07:33,501 --> 00:07:37,500
And there was some romance and adventure in that idea.

142
00:07:39,080 --> 00:07:41,920
And that romance peaked in the 1970s

143
00:07:41,921 --> 00:07:44,540
when trucker culture took off,

144
00:07:44,541 --> 00:07:48,620
leading to chart-topping songs like C.W. McCall's Convoy,

145
00:07:48,621 --> 00:07:51,780
high-grossing films like Smokey and the Bandit,

146
00:07:51,781 --> 00:07:56,380
And of course, trucker hats, which stuck around until the 2000s,

147
00:07:56,381 --> 00:08:01,020
but are making a comeback as we speak.

148
00:08:01,021 --> 00:08:05,020
Today, 70% of our goods are transported via truck.

149
00:08:05,021 --> 00:08:10,060
And if long-haul truckers stopped driving, within 24 hours,

150
00:08:10,061 --> 00:08:13,060
hospitals would start running out of basic supplies.

151
00:08:13,061 --> 00:08:16,380
And within three days, most grocery stores

152
00:08:16,381 --> 00:08:18,540
would start running out of food.

153
00:08:18,541 --> 00:08:21,880
And ATMs would be out of cash.

154
00:08:24,720 --> 00:08:26,880
But as long as Daimler is here, we'll

155
00:08:26,881 --> 00:08:29,420
be able to count on their freight liners.

156
00:08:29,421 --> 00:08:32,220
I'm starting off at the beginning of the line

157
00:08:32,221 --> 00:08:35,020
in the chassis department.

158
00:08:35,021 --> 00:08:39,100
The chassis are basically the spine of the trucks.

159
00:08:39,101 --> 00:08:44,100
This is where critical pieces, such as the axles, brakes,

160
00:08:44,101 --> 00:08:47,560
and the air compression tanks for the braking system

161
00:08:47,561 --> 00:08:48,820
are installed.

162
00:08:48,821 --> 00:08:50,200
So we're bringing the chassis in.

163
00:08:50,201 --> 00:08:53,600
Station one, we install all the internal cross members.

164
00:08:53,601 --> 00:08:56,240
That gives the strength to the truck.

165
00:08:56,241 --> 00:09:00,600
It takes a minimum of 353 workers

166
00:09:00,601 --> 00:09:03,740
to operate this part of the assembly line.

167
00:09:03,741 --> 00:09:07,520
And because the line itself is in constant motion,

168
00:09:07,521 --> 00:09:09,980
they are in constant motion, too.

169
00:09:09,981 --> 00:09:13,220
The chassis moves down the line nonstop

170
00:09:13,221 --> 00:09:15,160
at half a mile an hour.

171
00:09:15,161 --> 00:09:19,800
I noticed you have these big old chains hanging down here.

172
00:09:19,801 --> 00:09:22,500
What you're referring to here, that's called the flipper.

173
00:09:22,501 --> 00:09:25,000
Oh, my.

174
00:09:25,001 --> 00:09:27,000
It's a truck rotisserie.

175
00:09:31,180 --> 00:09:34,380
There are close to 16 million trucks operating

176
00:09:34,381 --> 00:09:37,440
on America's roadways today, enough to reach the moon

177
00:09:37,441 --> 00:09:39,080
if you lined each of them up.

178
00:09:39,081 --> 00:09:42,360
And I'm at Dyneler's largest truck factory,

179
00:09:42,361 --> 00:09:45,160
checking out how the most common of these long haulers

180
00:09:45,161 --> 00:09:47,160
is me.

181
00:09:47,161 --> 00:09:49,660
Oh, my.

182
00:09:49,661 --> 00:09:52,500
It's a truck rotisserie.

183
00:09:52,501 --> 00:09:53,960
This is insane.

184
00:09:53,961 --> 00:09:56,000
So as you can see, the chassis is currently

185
00:09:56,001 --> 00:09:56,900
built upside down.

186
00:09:56,901 --> 00:09:57,900
We built it inverted.

187
00:09:57,901 --> 00:09:59,300
I didn't even realize.

188
00:09:59,301 --> 00:10:01,600
That's ergonomically friendly for the employees.

189
00:10:01,601 --> 00:10:04,220
I see.

190
00:10:04,221 --> 00:10:06,220
Depending on what the customer wants,

191
00:10:06,221 --> 00:10:10,220
the chassis can be made short or long, potentially up

192
00:10:10,221 --> 00:10:12,760
to 40 feet in length.

193
00:10:12,761 --> 00:10:16,560
The number of cross members can range from five to eight,

194
00:10:16,561 --> 00:10:21,640
and axles have a minimum of two to a maximum of seven

195
00:10:21,641 --> 00:10:24,100
that can come with different braking systems

196
00:10:24,101 --> 00:10:27,440
like drum or disc.

197
00:10:27,441 --> 00:10:30,120
To think that each bolt represents work done

198
00:10:30,121 --> 00:10:34,940
by a Daimler worker, that is absolutely insane.

199
00:10:34,941 --> 00:10:36,620
I know that you have a lot of work.

200
00:10:36,621 --> 00:10:37,860
You are the man.

201
00:10:37,861 --> 00:10:39,080
I'm going to head on down the line.

202
00:10:39,081 --> 00:10:39,980
Bye.

203
00:10:42,220 --> 00:10:44,780
Whether it's behind the wheel of a Freightliner

204
00:10:44,781 --> 00:10:48,180
or your family car, driving is a huge part

205
00:10:48,181 --> 00:10:49,800
of American culture.

206
00:10:49,801 --> 00:10:52,860
Future technology is coming for our cars as well,

207
00:10:52,861 --> 00:10:58,460
starting with driverless cars, which actually got their start

208
00:10:58,461 --> 00:11:01,260
on our moon.

209
00:11:01,261 --> 00:11:04,100
Real autonomy started in the space

210
00:11:04,101 --> 00:11:08,540
age where lunar vehicles were being experimented with

211
00:11:08,541 --> 00:11:12,020
And there needed to be some sort of solution

212
00:11:12,021 --> 00:11:15,080
from an engineering standpoint to account for the 2 and 1

213
00:11:15,081 --> 00:11:17,660
half second delay between Earth and the moon.

214
00:11:17,661 --> 00:11:19,400
So these lunar rovers were designed

215
00:11:19,401 --> 00:11:22,880
to move autonomously in their environment.

216
00:11:22,881 --> 00:11:25,760
And today, back here on Earth, we're

217
00:11:25,761 --> 00:11:28,640
now looking at a future where we all could be chauffeured

218
00:11:28,641 --> 00:11:30,920
around by our cars.

219
00:11:30,921 --> 00:11:34,460
And more autonomous vehicles means less traffic,

220
00:11:34,461 --> 00:11:38,040
because they are built to communicate with each other

221
00:11:38,041 --> 00:11:41,480
to eventually eliminate things like traffic jams

222
00:11:41,481 --> 00:11:44,180
and bottlenecks on our roadways.

223
00:11:44,181 --> 00:11:47,140
The number one advantage is safety.

224
00:11:47,141 --> 00:11:49,980
90 plus percent of all traffic accidents

225
00:11:49,981 --> 00:11:52,120
are related to human error.

226
00:11:52,121 --> 00:11:55,820
And so autonomous vehicles are going to eliminate that

227
00:11:55,821 --> 00:11:57,300
for the most part.

228
00:11:57,301 --> 00:12:00,000
While we might have to wait another 10 years or more

229
00:12:00,001 --> 00:12:03,540
before autonomous vehicles are part of our day-to-day reality,

230
00:12:03,541 --> 00:12:07,800
when they do arrive, their impact could be huge.

231
00:12:07,801 --> 00:12:12,300
researchers have predicted they could save hundreds of thousands

232
00:12:12,301 --> 00:12:15,540
of lives over the course of a decade.

233
00:12:15,541 --> 00:12:19,260
But what innovation could top driverless cars?

234
00:12:19,261 --> 00:12:21,540
How about flying cars?

235
00:12:25,200 --> 00:12:28,140
Earlier this year, a real-life flying car

236
00:12:28,141 --> 00:12:31,700
was tested in Europe that can convert from an automobile

237
00:12:31,701 --> 00:12:34,700
to a plane in under three minutes.

238
00:12:34,701 --> 00:12:37,380
So what used to be science fiction

239
00:12:37,381 --> 00:12:41,280
could become part of our transportation future.

240
00:12:41,281 --> 00:12:44,300
And that future can't come soon enough

241
00:12:44,301 --> 00:12:48,700
because daily commutes are up since the 80s,

242
00:12:48,701 --> 00:12:53,460
from around 22 minutes to almost a half hour each way,

243
00:12:53,461 --> 00:12:54,500
which adds up.

244
00:12:54,501 --> 00:12:56,720
Each year, we lose the equivalent

245
00:12:56,721 --> 00:13:01,480
of about one full-time work week to our commutes.

246
00:13:01,481 --> 00:13:05,420
But in this nondescript 20,000 square foot facility,

247
00:13:07,920 --> 00:13:11,300
one company is hoping to change the way we get around

248
00:13:11,301 --> 00:13:15,060
by bringing flying to the masses.

249
00:13:15,061 --> 00:13:18,260
Most people give up on that dream

250
00:13:18,261 --> 00:13:20,540
of actually owning or even flying helicopters

251
00:13:20,541 --> 00:13:22,000
because it's very expensive.

252
00:13:22,001 --> 00:13:23,900
So our goal was in the beginning

253
00:13:23,901 --> 00:13:26,140
to create a portable helicopter

254
00:13:26,141 --> 00:13:29,380
where the average guy can own your own helicopter

255
00:13:29,381 --> 00:13:30,700
and fly it.

256
00:13:30,701 --> 00:13:33,640
And that's a game changer.

257
00:13:33,641 --> 00:13:35,500
Brand new traditional helicopters

258
00:13:35,501 --> 00:13:40,980
can range from $300,000 to up into the millions.

259
00:13:40,981 --> 00:13:43,720
Most of us probably can't afford that.

260
00:13:43,721 --> 00:13:47,700
But these mini machines cost less than half that,

261
00:13:47,701 --> 00:13:50,220
starting at just $51,000.

262
00:13:50,221 --> 00:13:54,100
This business started as a side business in the late 90s.

263
