00:28:09,780 --> 00:28:11,780
for Gottlieb Daimler.

516
00:28:13,780 --> 00:28:18,780
In 1885, the very first motorcycle was invented by...

517
00:28:18,781 --> 00:28:22,780
Gottlieb Daimler, by putting a combustible engine

518
00:28:22,781 --> 00:28:24,780
on a wooden bike frame.

519
00:28:24,781 --> 00:28:29,780
America's trucking industry employs over 8 million people

520
00:28:29,781 --> 00:28:32,780
that work hard to make sure that our cargo

521
00:28:32,781 --> 00:28:36,780
has an on-time delivery rating of 95% or more.

522
00:28:36,781 --> 00:28:39,780
But to be able to deliver our goods so promptly,

523
00:28:39,880 --> 00:28:43,080
truck drivers and their rigs have to operate

524
00:28:43,180 --> 00:28:46,860
under some extreme conditions when they need to make miles.

525
00:28:46,960 --> 00:28:50,720
From notoriously icy roads in North Dakota,

526
00:28:50,820 --> 00:28:55,700
high winds in Wyoming, and flash flood plagues Texas,

527
00:28:55,800 --> 00:28:58,640
these trucks have to withstand it all.

528
00:28:58,740 --> 00:29:02,740
And this machine, called the Water Halo Test,

529
00:29:02,840 --> 00:29:05,700
is built to put the weatherization of these trucks

530
00:29:05,701 --> 00:29:07,700
trucks through its paces.

531
00:29:07,701 --> 00:29:10,700
Testing the pop and the sides and all the seams,

532
00:29:10,701 --> 00:29:12,040
the doors, all that.

533
00:29:12,041 --> 00:29:14,580
So it's like if you're driving a torrential downpour.

534
00:29:14,581 --> 00:29:15,540
Exactly.

535
00:29:15,541 --> 00:29:17,580
This is the worst storm you would ever, ever

536
00:29:17,581 --> 00:29:19,420
want to drive through.

537
00:29:19,421 --> 00:29:20,580
Please hold.

538
00:29:20,581 --> 00:29:22,920
Please hold.

539
00:29:22,921 --> 00:29:24,960
The whole truck is rumbling.

540
00:29:24,961 --> 00:29:27,380
Like it's literally shaking the truck.

541
00:29:27,381 --> 00:29:30,160
And these things weigh tons.

542
00:29:30,161 --> 00:29:32,020
So I guess this is a pass?

543
00:29:32,021 --> 00:29:33,020
This is a pass.

544
00:29:33,021 --> 00:29:33,920
That was amazing.

545
00:29:35,700 --> 00:29:38,540
This and the rest of Daimler's latest trucks

546
00:29:38,640 --> 00:29:41,940
that roll off the line today will join the fleet of trucks

547
00:29:42,040 --> 00:29:45,540
dedicated to moving our goods across the country.

548
00:29:45,640 --> 00:29:48,240
But before there were trucks to help transport the things

549
00:29:48,340 --> 00:29:51,440
we buy, there were trains.

550
00:29:51,540 --> 00:29:54,660
TRAINS totally shaped the history of the United States

551
00:29:54,760 --> 00:29:57,660
because they allowed goods to be transported for the first time

552
00:29:57,760 --> 00:30:00,360
across the entire country.

553
00:30:00,460 --> 00:30:03,360
Starting with steam engines in the 1800s,

554
00:30:03,361 --> 00:30:09,860
locomotives gradually updated from steam to diesel to electric.

555
00:30:09,861 --> 00:30:12,860
Currently, the fastest passenger train in America

556
00:30:12,861 --> 00:30:18,700
is Amtrak's Acela, which can reach speeds of 150 miles an hour.

557
00:30:18,701 --> 00:30:22,780
And all the way across the world, there's a train that's even faster,

558
00:30:22,781 --> 00:30:24,520
the Maglev.

559
00:30:24,521 --> 00:30:26,320
Maglev trains don't have wheels.

560
00:30:26,321 --> 00:30:29,860
Instead, they have a set of extremely powerful magnets.

561
00:30:29,861 --> 00:30:31,860
There's one set of magnets in the train

562
00:30:31,861 --> 00:30:34,020
and another set of magnets in the track.

563
00:30:34,120 --> 00:30:35,900
Those magnets repel each other,

564
00:30:36,000 --> 00:30:37,700
and that actually levitates the train

565
00:30:37,800 --> 00:30:39,660
just a little bit off the track.

566
00:30:39,760 --> 00:30:43,000
And that allows you to reduce the slowing force of friction

567
00:30:43,100 --> 00:30:45,600
so that the train can go really fast.

568
00:30:45,700 --> 00:30:48,040
And we do mean fast.

569
00:30:48,140 --> 00:30:49,940
The fastest maglev train in the world

570
00:30:50,040 --> 00:30:54,380
topped out at 375 miles per hour.

571
00:30:54,480 --> 00:30:57,220
That's fast enough to get you from Miami to New York

572
00:30:57,320 --> 00:30:59,380
in under four hours.

573
00:30:59,381 --> 00:31:02,880
But there's potentially an even more futuristic

574
00:31:02,881 --> 00:31:07,380
and faster train in the works here in the U.S.

575
00:31:11,720 --> 00:31:14,220
While Maglev eliminates the friction

576
00:31:14,221 --> 00:31:16,800
between a train's wheels and the track,

577
00:31:16,801 --> 00:31:20,460
the train is still slowed by friction with the air around it.

578
00:31:20,461 --> 00:31:24,380
So the idea behind Hyperloop is to eliminate that air.

579
00:31:24,381 --> 00:31:26,880
Pods carrying passengers would travel

580
00:31:26,881 --> 00:31:30,420
through huge vacuum tunnels that have had most of their air

581
00:31:30,421 --> 00:31:31,420
removed.

582
00:31:31,421 --> 00:31:34,080
And eventually, the Hyperloop's pods will be able to travel

583
00:31:34,081 --> 00:31:36,720
at 670 miles per hour.

584
00:31:36,721 --> 00:31:39,420
Originally the brainchild of Elon Musk,

585
00:31:39,421 --> 00:31:42,420
Virgin has since taken over development

586
00:31:42,421 --> 00:31:45,040
and plans to launch in 2030.

587
00:31:45,041 --> 00:31:47,840
So by then, at the speeds we're talking about,

588
00:31:47,841 --> 00:31:52,880
you could travel between New York and DC in 20 minutes.

589
00:31:52,881 --> 00:31:55,380
But even if our freight railways never

590
00:31:55,381 --> 00:31:57,620
never reach those kinds of speeds,

591
00:31:57,621 --> 00:32:01,280
many still argue they're the best in the world,

592
00:32:01,281 --> 00:32:07,620
covering 140,000 route miles, by far the most

593
00:32:07,621 --> 00:32:10,220
anywhere around the globe.

594
00:32:10,221 --> 00:32:12,700
Out west, no other American railroad

595
00:32:12,701 --> 00:32:15,540
better embodies the might of our railways

596
00:32:15,541 --> 00:32:17,900
than Union Pacific.

597
00:32:17,901 --> 00:32:21,600
Chartered in 1862, when President Abraham Lincoln

598
00:32:21,601 --> 00:32:24,580
signed the Pacific Railway Act with the goal

599
00:32:24,581 --> 00:32:28,660
of connecting the West with the rest of the country by rail.

600
00:32:28,760 --> 00:32:33,320
And the Transcontinental Railroad was completed in 1869,

601
00:32:33,420 --> 00:32:36,820
when Union Pacific's line connected to Central Pacific's

602
00:32:36,920 --> 00:32:42,660
line in Utah, creating a boon of trade and population growth

603
00:32:42,760 --> 00:32:46,160
beyond the eastern seaboard.

604
00:32:46,260 --> 00:32:49,980
Today, across two different shops along Union Pacific's line,

605
00:32:50,080 --> 00:32:52,900
they modernize their current fleet for the future,

606
00:32:52,901 --> 00:32:57,100
while also simultaneously operating their iconic Big Boy

607
00:32:57,101 --> 00:33:01,600
locomotive, one of the last that is still able to ride

608
00:33:01,601 --> 00:33:03,640
the rails.

609
00:33:03,641 --> 00:33:06,940
The Big Boy locomotive were referred to as the 4000

610
00:33:06,941 --> 00:33:08,040
class.

611
00:33:08,041 --> 00:33:12,860
They were just right around 610 tons, 133 feet long,

612
00:33:12,861 --> 00:33:16,260
almost 17 feet tall, 11 and 1 1⁄2 feet wide.

613
00:33:16,261 --> 00:33:19,700
The modern diesel locomotive is 74 feet long,

614
00:33:19,701 --> 00:33:22,400
and just a little over 200 tons.

615
00:33:22,401 --> 00:33:24,900
There were 25 big boys built exclusively

616
00:33:24,901 --> 00:33:27,780
for Union Pacific Railroad, the first of which

617
00:33:27,781 --> 00:33:32,780
was delivered in 1941, but only eight remain today.

618
00:33:32,781 --> 00:33:36,280
They were built to be able to tow long freight lines

619
00:33:36,281 --> 00:33:42,260
with as many as 125 cars, collectively weighing 3,800

620
00:33:42,261 --> 00:33:46,000
tons, all in one shot over the Wasatch Mountain Range

621
00:33:46,001 --> 00:33:47,360
in Utah.

622
00:33:47,361 --> 00:33:50,160
At that point, that was a game changer.

623
00:33:50,161 --> 00:33:53,960
The railroad was faced with serious congestion.

624
00:33:53,961 --> 00:33:57,900
There was no way for them to build additional tracks.

625
00:33:57,901 --> 00:34:02,640
They had to find a way to move that amount of tonnage.

626
00:34:02,641 --> 00:34:06,240
And the only solution was to make a bigger, stronger

627
00:34:06,241 --> 00:34:07,540
locomotive.

628
00:34:07,541 --> 00:34:09,980
That's what led to the development of the Big Boy

629
00:34:09,981 --> 00:34:13,020
locomotive.

630
00:34:13,021 --> 00:34:15,980
During World War II, the Big Boys were massively

631
00:34:15,981 --> 00:34:19,580
important in moving materials for the war effort and our

632
00:34:19,581 --> 00:34:22,920
are acclaimed for their wartime contributions.

633
00:34:22,921 --> 00:34:27,380
After the war, the big boys ran until 1961,

634
00:34:27,381 --> 00:34:30,460
making them one of the last steam locomotives

635
00:34:30,461 --> 00:34:32,820
to go out of service.

636
00:34:32,821 --> 00:34:34,740
And what replaced them?

637
00:34:34,741 --> 00:34:36,040
Diesel engines.

638
00:34:36,041 --> 00:34:38,020
And further down Union Pacific's line

639
00:34:38,021 --> 00:34:41,880
is the shop where a team of 175 people

640
00:34:41,881 --> 00:34:46,080
do some literal heavy lifting to keep their active fleet

641
00:34:46,081 --> 00:34:50,660
of more than 4,600 locomotives operational,

642
00:34:50,661 --> 00:34:54,420
while adding a touch of modern innovation as well.

643
00:34:54,421 --> 00:34:57,040
The first thing we do when we bring the unit into the shop

644
00:34:57,041 --> 00:34:59,660
is remove the components that need to come out

645
00:34:59,661 --> 00:35:02,200
to be replaced while it's in the shop.

646
00:35:02,201 --> 00:35:04,220
The most impressive piece of equipment we have

647
00:35:04,221 --> 00:35:07,640
is the 250-ton crane, which can lift the entire locomotive.

648
00:35:07,641 --> 00:35:11,760
Diesel locomotives can weigh more than 200 tons.

649
00:35:11,761 --> 00:35:14,420
That's heavier than the Statue of Liberty.

650
00:35:14,421 --> 00:35:18,720
So this crane has to use nearly all of its lifting power.

651
00:35:18,721 --> 00:35:21,540
And the most important upgrade these trains get

652
00:35:21,541 --> 00:35:24,860
brings them into the 21st century.

653
00:35:24,861 --> 00:35:27,420
PTC stands for Positive Train Control

654
00:35:27,421 --> 00:35:31,220
and has given us the ability to know where our trains were

655
00:35:31,221 --> 00:35:33,340
anywhere in the country at all times.

656
00:35:33,341 --> 00:35:36,280
Positive Train Control uses technologies

657
00:35:36,281 --> 00:35:38,620
across the telecommunication spectrum

658
00:35:38,621 --> 00:35:41,820
to monitor each train's location, speed,

659
00:35:41,821 --> 00:35:44,420
and distance from potential hazards,

660
00:35:44,421 --> 00:35:48,460
and can pinpoint where trains are within 11 feet

661
00:35:48,461 --> 00:35:51,520
of their actual location in real time.

662
00:35:51,521 --> 00:35:54,880
But its biggest benefit is safety,

663
00:35:54,881 --> 00:35:57,880
since the technology can automatically slow

664
00:35:57,881 --> 00:36:02,880
or stop the train to avoid collisions or derailments.

665
00:36:02,881 --> 00:36:04,520
America needs us.

666
00:36:04,521 --> 00:36:08,860
There's no way goods get from point A to point B without us.

667
00:36:08,861 --> 00:36:12,420
And so what we build, what we put back together,

668
00:36:12,421 --> 00:36:16,400
gets those goods from point A to point B without fail.

669
00:36:19,100 --> 00:36:21,980
Planes, trains, and automobiles.

670
00:36:21,981 --> 00:36:25,060
By now, we're familiar with these common modes of transport.

671
00:36:25,061 --> 00:36:27,620
Each day, roughly 100,000 flights

672
00:36:27,621 --> 00:36:29,660
take off and land around the world.

673
00:36:29,661 --> 00:36:33,520
Amtrak can transport nearly 90,000 passengers,

674
00:36:33,521 --> 00:36:38,500
and automobiles have consumed as much as 392 million

675
00:36:38,501 --> 00:36:42,040
gallons of fuel, a record in 2018.

676
00:36:42,041 --> 00:36:45,700
But throughout history, all the way up to present day,

677
00:36:45,701 --> 00:36:48,220
you can still find some unique ways

678
00:36:48,221 --> 00:36:51,580
that people get around across the globe.

679
00:36:51,581 --> 00:36:54,540
In North America, dog sleds were once commonplace

680
00:36:54,541 --> 00:36:58,300
as the most effective means of crossing snow-covered terrain.

681
00:36:58,301 --> 00:37:01,780
And they proved their worth in 1925,

682
00:37:01,781 --> 00:37:04,900
when they came through to deliver critical medicine

683
00:37:04,901 --> 00:37:07,780
to an epidemic-stricken town in Alaska.

684
00:37:07,781 --> 00:37:10,740
It was in the dead of winter, so the best thing they could do

685
00:37:10,741 --> 00:37:13,200
is set up a dog sledding relay

686
00:37:13,201 --> 00:37:16,860
that would begin a 700-mile trip to Nome.

687
00:37:16,861 --> 00:37:20,400
And that five-day relay brought medical supplies,

688
00:37:20,401 --> 00:37:22,940
which treated people that had the outbreak

689
00:37:22,941 --> 00:37:26,580
and saved many lives.

690
00:37:26,581 --> 00:37:28,900
Across the Pacific from Alaska,

691
00:37:28,901 --> 00:37:33,320
the jeepney is the most popular form of transport in Manila,

692
00:37:33,321 --> 00:37:35,740
the largest city in the Philippines.

693
00:37:35,741 --> 00:37:41,580
If it looks somewhat familiar, it might be because they got their start after World War II,

694
00:37:41,880 --> 00:37:47,000
when U.S. troops left behind Jeeps that were recycled as buses.

695
00:37:47,540 --> 00:37:51,080
The interesting part is they have regular routes, but they don't have regular stops.

696
00:37:51,480 --> 00:37:54,860
So you actually have to tell the driver, I'm stopping here.

697
00:37:54,861 --> 00:37:59,460
And actually to get in and out of the Jeepneys, they put the benches side to side.

698
00:37:59,560 --> 00:38:03,960
Since it was sort of a quick on and quick off, they had to keep that aisle clear.

699
00:38:03,961 --> 00:38:07,960
But over in the UK, something straight out of James Bond

700
00:38:08,060 --> 00:38:09,960
has been shuffling British citizens

701
00:38:10,060 --> 00:38:13,960
between Portsmouth and the Isle of Wight since 1965,

702
00:38:14,060 --> 00:38:19,000
the only commercial passenger hovercraft in the world.

703
00:38:19,100 --> 00:38:22,720
The hovercraft is the best way to travel over the solar

704
00:38:22,820 --> 00:38:25,720
because the tide is maybe half a mile sometimes.

705
00:38:25,820 --> 00:38:29,220
It actually shoots air below it to create an air cushion.

706
00:38:29,320 --> 00:38:33,120
So the hovercraft is a great way to move over both land

707
00:38:33,121 --> 00:38:37,420
land and water, and it's an iconic way of traveling in England.

708
00:38:38,580 --> 00:38:42,640
But no matter how humans have gotten from place to place through the years,

709
00:38:43,020 --> 00:38:49,920
today, one of the most trusted and time-tested methods for moving cargo around on the road

710
00:38:49,921 --> 00:38:51,260
is trucks.

711
00:38:51,820 --> 00:38:56,700
There are around 3.5 million truck drivers that share the road with us today,

712
00:38:56,880 --> 00:39:02,940
and I am finally about to get a taste of how the Freightliner makes their journeys

713
00:39:02,941 --> 00:39:07,680
as easy as possible for those lucky enough to drive one.

714
00:39:07,681 --> 00:39:09,740
Gee, just look at the size of this.

715
00:39:09,741 --> 00:39:13,980
I mean, you're used to it, but seeing a bunch of pieces

716
00:39:13,981 --> 00:39:17,080
come together, it was like a puzzle six hours ago.

717
00:39:17,081 --> 00:39:17,980
Absolutely.

718
00:39:17,981 --> 00:39:19,420
And now it's a freightliner.

719
00:39:19,421 --> 00:39:22,160
In a matter of hours, I'm standing

720
00:39:22,161 --> 00:39:26,560
in front of a completed road-ready freightliner.

721
00:39:26,561 --> 00:39:31,300
These cabs alone can weigh up to 20,000 pounds.

722
00:39:31,301 --> 00:39:34,100
But fully loaded, trailer and all,

723
00:39:34,101 --> 00:39:38,800
they can weigh as much as 80,000 pounds.

724
00:39:38,801 --> 00:39:40,300
Oh, my god.

725
00:39:40,301 --> 00:39:43,040
I am really the king of the road now.

726
00:39:43,041 --> 00:39:45,040
Oh, it's like a house.

727
00:39:45,041 --> 00:39:46,940
It's actually bigger than most New York apartments.

728
00:39:46,941 --> 00:39:49,120
This is amazing.

729
00:39:49,121 --> 00:39:52,980
Most truck drivers cover an average of 2,500 miles

730
00:39:52,981 --> 00:39:56,860
on the road each week, which adds up to an average

731
00:39:56,861 --> 00:40:00,900
of 125,000 miles per year.

732
00:40:00,901 --> 00:40:05,280
So it's not hard to see why a spacious cab comes in handy.

733
00:40:08,580 --> 00:40:10,080
Woo!

734
00:40:10,081 --> 00:40:11,380
Flip that.

735
00:40:11,381 --> 00:40:13,880
So now you're in drive.

736
00:40:13,881 --> 00:40:15,080
Now you gotta take the parking brake.

737
00:40:15,081 --> 00:40:16,620
What, you're going to be driving?

738
00:40:16,621 --> 00:40:18,260
Absolutely, you're driving.

739
00:40:18,261 --> 00:40:19,480
And this is awesome.

740
00:40:19,481 --> 00:40:20,380
That's the horn, yeah?

741
00:40:20,381 --> 00:40:21,420
That's the horn.

742
00:40:21,421 --> 00:40:24,460
I have always, always wanted to do this.

743
00:40:28,060 --> 00:40:30,400
Oh, that felt amazing.

744
00:40:30,401 --> 00:40:32,200
Oh, my God.

745
00:40:32,201 --> 00:40:35,000
This is just amazing.

746
00:40:35,001 --> 00:40:36,300
Mm-mm.

747
00:40:36,301 --> 00:40:38,700
I can feel it automatically shifting for me, for sure.

748
00:40:38,701 --> 00:40:41,880
So, yeah, there's an automated manual transmission shift.

749
00:40:41,881 --> 00:40:45,020
It knows how fast we're going, what the engine RPMs are.

750
00:40:45,021 --> 00:40:48,920
It's picking the right gear to be in for what we're doing.

751
00:40:48,921 --> 00:40:52,660
This truck is smarter than most people I know.

752
00:40:52,661 --> 00:40:54,360
I don't know what I expected when I finally

753
00:40:54,361 --> 00:40:57,420
got my chance of living out the dreams of driving a truck,

754
00:40:57,421 --> 00:41:00,600
But I'm glad that it was a Freightliner for the first time.

755
00:41:00,601 --> 00:41:04,260
Because, man, this is awesome.

756
00:41:04,261 --> 00:41:05,960
Fantastic.

757
00:41:05,961 --> 00:41:08,760
This morning, this truck didn't exist.

758
00:41:08,761 --> 00:41:12,800
And now, it's this incredible modern marvel of engineering.

759
00:41:12,801 --> 00:41:16,580
But there is always a new horizon up ahead.

760
00:41:16,581 --> 00:41:19,900
And the long road awaits.

761
00:41:19,901 --> 00:41:21,840
So get moving, America.

762
00:41:21,841 --> 00:41:22,840
See you next time.

763
00:41:27,420 --> 00:41:29,420
You

